    <h2 style="margin-top:3rem">What the walk-through covers</h2>
    <p>When the video lands, it walks through these in order. You can mentally walk through them now using the demo sites at <a href="/samples/" class="inline-link">/samples/</a>:</p>
    <ol class="walkthrough-list">
      <li>
        <strong>The homepage.</strong>
        Hero, value prop, trust signals, a call-to-action above the fold. What loads, in what order, and how fast.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A service page.</strong>
        How a single service is structured: outcome-focused headline, what is included, the three questions a customer is actually asking, schema markup that wires it to the local pack.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A service-area page.</strong>
        Why one real city page beats ten doorway pages: real local content, local map, references to the neighborhoods you actually work in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The contact form.</strong>
        Trade-specific intake (not Name/Email/Message): the four or five questions that pre-qualify a lead before it ever hits your inbox.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The pricing page.</strong>
        How transparent pricing changes the conversation with the customer. Why posted prices increase qualified inquiries instead of decreasing them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The blog.</strong>
        Optional. How a blog earns long-tail organic traffic over time, and how the maintenance burden actually breaks down.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The dark mode toggle, the search, the sticky table of contents, the analytics dashboard.</strong>
        Quality-of-life features that take a site from "passable" to "actually used."
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Lighthouse run.</strong>
        Live PageSpeed score on every page, in front of you. Most owners have never seen a 95+ run on their own URL; once they do, the comparison is hard to un-see.
      </li>
    </ol>

